#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133460 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83399378-8815-5602-a60a-6901114416f7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the General Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "e16376f2-ffb9-580c-a7e7-57f5814f8289" :question/math-academy-id "e-21137" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/c49d8a92f1fa8e94c78250d9b4aa771ef97e322603be667d3be98aa25c82cf81.png)\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/e2e235f7970c2f644302c2f119837b14dc5e6f6a94969c53f92c0193f6aca629.png)\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/729acfdc97d8800458a057a419b8efdcb5d9087af3374125cfb293584e47394a.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=7 \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c_1= 5 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_1 = 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n1 - c_2 = -1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_2 = 2.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "d2768264-6d1a-5e62-9af4-025c877e138b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5608" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/e3f6d6e26983db7b894d0bb2ba09e66fdc55edecb0d9bc9d8a35821449707b7e.png)\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=-3.$*" :question/worked-solution "Let's construct the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/4790200622ff45c885147ca0c897eefcbb4bdcefe10d67b8960cc167c3b2cccf.png)\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= 2\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2= -3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =2,$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/10bbcec99a48d331c21836dc104a1db2db241e50a534baf0db23c138168c5663.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/c2bff2faa79d780c5f89b28e72468c84a388d50e8c566b80e5d5677f90a5e3af.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =-3,$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/704493d5ac4f48c88a9c521c4e43c41acfd80b6d6959237944fbc0d638e6012f.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/a77312d93a804e69dabb70aff5bc7bbf9798781c728dfc50fff1440da86b5b5c.png)\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution and use the initial values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $c_2 = -1$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-1=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =6[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133535 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "226a5f8f-ac26-5f3e-8f8f-f145c314820f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5609" :question/problem "Let $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=-2$ be eigenvalues of the $2\\times 2$ matrix  $A$ that correspond to eigenvectors \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/15d8764ce12b985a991ca9a0704ba037ccc29fa488a3f23ee643688ceebae60c.png) \nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ and that $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067494/assets/88cd1c87501899a5d6b49ce3fd1678fe9485300a2da638fb496f2c80b298ea86.png)\nfind the position of the particle after $2$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ -2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-11 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n16c_1 = -32\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_1=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2\\cdot(-2) -3c_2 = 1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nc_2=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $\\mathbf x(2)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\cdot 2}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12{e}^{6}+{e}^{-4} \\\\ 4{e}^{6}-3{e}^{-4}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd823ee5-24d1-5ddf-8fcb-166f0bdddcc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355627} {:answer/id #uuid "41d7047c-707d-526d-89cc-4bce96113e37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4}+3{e}^{6} \\\\ -{e}^{-4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355628} {:answer/id #uuid "df272c2a-7644-5424-a875-dda4bbbe7d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4} \\\\ 5{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355629} {:answer/id #uuid "01ed4800-cb6d-532b-801e-4e798905c0ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ {e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355630} {:answer/id #uuid "54094274-b30a-5c0c-9eb6-fe047f22b4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{6} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4}+{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd823ee5-24d1-5ddf-8fcb-166f0bdddcc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355627} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e47b642-1b03-51e6-a0ef-cf4d69db23c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "048c4d19-f776-56af-aac6-30cdbc559443" :question/math-academy-id "q-168917" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=2$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2(0)}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3(0)} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=3 \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n4{c}_{1}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-1+{c}_{2}=3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2(2)}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3(2)} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97cc6199-21cb-522f-a522-08aa78ff7c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355633} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd779f7-789e-513f-b7c1-5c2e204ef463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355634} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9a9a1b-ea05-55b6-b452-f51a65d09616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355635} {:answer/id #uuid "d4180209-eb1b-5e47-8178-b5952244006b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355636} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d93c16-ef34-5cee-800d-a54b26ea8552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c9a9a1b-ea05-55b6-b452-f51a65d09616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355635} :answer-field/id #uuid "b62378ea-f5db-5477-8c28-5399c7b8fd2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2f46aeb-4fa8-5261-bc96-7f74682a5de2" :question/math-academy-id "q-324462" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4(0)}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-0} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 5{c}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=2.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=1.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83399378-8815-5602-a60a-6901114416f7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2a195d6-34a2-5a6d-b4a7-3accd27b383c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2a195d6-34a2-5a6d-b4a7-3accd27b383c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355642} :answer-field/id #uuid "c29b3580-d3ff-56be-8bef-8adef14cd13b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131122} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9174d6b4-949e-5e49-ad6d-24f014506686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9174d6b4-949e-5e49-ad6d-24f014506686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355641} :answer-field/id #uuid "edb03679-939f-50ad-9fbe-8401c388d541" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131123} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "330d9960-10c9-5996-a6b3-f6419cadfb3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "330d9960-10c9-5996-a6b3-f6419cadfb3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355640} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e67592-b151-5882-ba6b-a4bfb8feb39a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131124} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb486f53-5ec0-52e0-a53a-b4ec38c992ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355638} {:answer/id #uuid "529329e1-dc83-565e-b1db-6fc9f714b16a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb486f53-5ec0-52e0-a53a-b4ec38c992ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355638} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b42f8d2-a930-5d23-ae8f-6ced6b787d4c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0d346a2-b012-5c0d-b429-60020e32ca6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-326002" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-7{c}_{2}=-7\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+3\\cdot 1=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83399378-8815-5602-a60a-6901114416f7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76d06364-4251-5f5c-bf86-1694d467eba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/valu