:answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b965a1be-feff-59c2-b300-635c6df21227" :question/math-academy-id "q-326291" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ -{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=7 \\\\ -{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the result to the  first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3{c}_{2}=9\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{c}_{1}+3=1\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83399378-8815-5602-a60a-6901114416f7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee83f52-ab45-5b49-a8b5-8d3250ae60fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aee83f52-ab45-5b49-a8b5-8d3250ae60fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355655} :answer-field/id #uuid "372a2af1-82e6-59f1-954d-2c2d51ad9dfa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355654} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a136a70-b3a1-575c-b311-e551eea059f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a136a70-b3a1-575c-b311-e551eea059f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355657} :answer-field/id #uuid "491e7061-35f7-56f9-9ca7-ef346ec28a51" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355656} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2202219-0af3-5b78-a8a9-7e14f0d3d434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2202219-0af3-5b78-a8a9-7e14f0d3d434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355659} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4bbe060-5857-54ee-826b-896585644269" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355658} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f277b3-3b0c-5696-a11e-305bac244d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42f277b3-3b0c-5696-a11e-305bac244d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355661} :answer-field/id #uuid "12d78cb7-13bd-55b5-9308-8fdee0296020" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fd11da8-69ae-58b7-a146-e95c709d162a" :question/math-academy-id "q-326294" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5{c}_{1}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2\\cdot 1+{c}_{2}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83399378-8815-5602-a60a-6901114416f7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69a8b8b2-2aea-55fb-a36e-f86cc392580d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69a8b8b2-2aea-55fb-a36e-f86cc392580d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355664} :answer-field/id #uuid "a462bd57-ae87-5df1-9e7a-04291078a34b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355663} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55ad3313-b96f-57bb-baf6-108c197b7c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55ad3313-b96f-57bb-baf6-108c197b7c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355666} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ae83df5-dd29-5f8b-abf1-0aac87e1c4fa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355665} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89b30514-fcda-55a0-9e70-4da6d519939d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89b30514-fcda-55a0-9e70-4da6d519939d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355668} :answer-field/id #uuid "356187e8-d219-5b8f-b526-d6d0cf618bd1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b731faa-fdb5-50a9-917e-2fb3b10d5b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b731faa-fdb5-50a9-917e-2fb3b10d5b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355670} :answer-field/id #uuid "8abece07-8bac-5d5a-ae85-b306d5b725da" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e67f0fc-9212-572f-8ed9-268986a5352f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326295" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5{c}_{2}=10\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2\\cdot 2=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83399378-8815-5602-a60a-6901114416f7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f818f94e-c19e-55c0-b951-1805856f0866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f818f94e-c19e-55c0-b951-1805856f0866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355673} :answer-field/id #uuid "afead4a6-d911-5551-8bd2-142c6557e9c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355672} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c964c8d0-7f1c-5e63-9001-e7e6183c9bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c964c8d0-7f1c-5e63-9001-e7e6183c9bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355675} :answer-field/id #uuid "9391c61f-d19b-5e84-9128-888ea0defe72" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355674} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65a5fb58-eb5d-542b-873c-b8da7a856497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65a5fb58-eb5d-542b-873c-b8da7a856497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355677} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1d5139-0880-5277-b703-668a56cd01a2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355676} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c081379-c37f-54ce-ac8e-b3f53bd9e696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c081379-c37f-54ce-ac8e-b3f53bd9e696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355679} :answer-field/id #uuid "d862e68c-c414-5165-979d-ceb07028a035" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94376933-6ac5-5c91-9bc7-de6f6e3d21f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-326298" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=4 \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{c}_{1}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2+{c}_{2}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b00df50-0780-5999-b69f-75ac47dee284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\frac{9}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355682} {:answer/id #uuid "c8dda6e0-8852-5a4d-8ad8-b443596c0fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-\\frac{7}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355683} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe978c2-a2b9-5aad-9a8e-6d1bb46adab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-\\frac{9}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355684} {:answer/id #uuid "e19aaf73-8265-5e1d-8141-af0389a0b29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}-\\frac{7}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355685} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5e7375-75ea-5281-8ec4-1cc62fa4a9df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{7}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e19aaf73-8265-5e1d-8141-af0389a0b29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}-\\frac{7}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355685} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a128bf5-933f-5d9d-a540-9c376c84eb10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45696d2e-86e2-52d9-b755-e4c9e51775a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-326437" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}=1 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting into the second equation, we obtain ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{7}{2}.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}-\\frac{7}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36fc2244-c4a9-5ce0-8c3a-900a1f91647c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355689} {:answer/id #uuid "79f0f083-ecd5-53b8-acdf-1aef955c0809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=8[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355690} {:answer/id #uuid "a1375143-3704-5f19-9ef7-eb867f0f8fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}-8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355691} {:answer/id #uuid "f456ed89-d102-5e65-8852-af4346b0d4cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355692} {:answer/id #uuid "ec57dc24-c88a-555f-9cce-a026e4f0db2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec57dc24-c88a-555f-9cce-a026e4f0db2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355693} :answer-field/id #uuid "af2b7029-f8f3-5991-9596-b360edd42294" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdbca987-f320-5de4-ab14-d89b85df973b" :question/math-academy-id "q-327132" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-5 \\\\ {c}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=3.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=-8.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fc0cd15-26c6-542f-96de-0f23572c4137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-2{e}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-3e[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355696} {:answer/id #uuid "2f27af7a-99e8-549a-86bd-c8add2d2cd12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2e[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}-3{e}^{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355697} {:answer/id #uuid "e17793a9-ee12-5f6c-a0b0-648fc712dc8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=e[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-{e}^{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355698} {:answer/id #uuid "39352f4d-7b43-5377-a49a-ceffcd1de0a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2e[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{e}^{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355699} {:answer/id #uuid "8234ac2f-ef24-51a2-9745-6106106f8df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e17793a9-ee12-5f6c-a0b0-648fc712dc8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=e[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-{e}^{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355698} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb00aeb7-7462-55e2-9762-96fcdb85eaa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96d25510-4b02-51b1-a16b-31f7b1aede10" :question/math-academy-id "q-327147" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned},\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1/2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-2{e}^{4} \\\\ 2{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}={e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second one, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3}{c}_{1}=3{e}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=e$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3}(e)+{c}_{2}={e}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =e[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-{e}^{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c9dc34-d3b5-5eb9-9274-7a2b0cc32183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355703} {:answer/id #uuid "3031a969-cd10-5dd7-ae2d-5bc9724f03e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355704} {:answer/id #uuid "e5eb53ea-a141-512b-82a5-48a5ba5ae65f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355705} {:answer/id #uuid "237533a6-1f35-5329-8d03-7987d58bd91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355706} {:answer/id #uuid "d067f806-41db-5fd5-a465-a827fb3529b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}-4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "237533a6-1f35-5329-8d03-7987d58bd91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355706} :answer-field/id #uuid "d737e1ed-2300-51b3-8f57-3f55764d1637" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80eb2676-605e-582a-9599-b9d33bd803ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-327160" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1/4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2(0)} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ 4{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}=-3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e989a718-9f0d-504d-906d-ff2a25950e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355709} {:answer/id #uuid "89262b27-d6ef-5ee4-a9c8-3466bf5608db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355710} {:answer/id #uuid "d93c3086-1a6d-505e-9e1e-8dfdbfc9c398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355711} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d777ed-4249-5a10-b0a7-8206bf3417f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355712} {:answer/id #uuid "d123369d-4443-50ca-8838-ec171ee6c4bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d93c3086-1a6d-505e-9e1e-8dfdbfc9c398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355711} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0fed8e9-93c1-5c6f-8ff0-3b1602c93cbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9d3d68e-7221-5fb6-8c31-50910c28ab47" :question/math-academy-id "q-39240" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n6{c}_{2}=3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd0a0770-da4a-561d-bd57-30c98d1a8883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 + e^{10} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131229} {:answer/id #uuid "21382289-9115-54b0-8e67-1410d1ca0b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 + e^{5} \\\\ e^{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131230} {:answer/id #uuid "5d40d40f-7647-5e0e-b43c-22f0b0d200b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 + e^{10} \\\\ 1 + e^{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131231} {:answer/id #uuid "67edc4d0-8b71-500a-987a-2f86b6786363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 + e^{10} \\\\ e^{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131232} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb7d4d4-d73d-5265-a68f-0985152e831f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 + e^{2} \\\\ e^{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67edc4d0-8b71-500a-987a-2f86b6786363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 + e^{10} \\\\ e^{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131232} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3d5d17a-735a-5a9c-8f6f-919467e22dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1f184ac-fee2-55eb-900b-6eaef0d92cda" :question/math-academy-id "q-39241" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=5$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ 2{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(5)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(5) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+{e}^{10} \\\\ {e}^{10}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb24a4c-0639-529a-bdfb-ab7ba0ddd9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{28}+18{e}^{-7} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355716} {:answer/id #uuid "13c39644-6f03-5375-8c70-df629c3e745b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18{e}^{28}+24{e}^{-7} \\\\ -6{e}^{28}+18{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355717} {:answer/id #uuid "0130a953-0236-5275-b236-bbbb30e2c9b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18{e}^{7}+24{e}^{-7} \\\\ -18{e}^{7}+18{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355718} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2a6b45-fa0f-5be8-a458-7f177a22e497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18{e}^{28}+24{e}^{-7} \\\\ -18{e}^{28}+18{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355719} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0376c0-0c08-550b-8d05-52426e5af069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12{e}^{28}+28{e}^{-7} \\\\ -18{e}^{28}+16{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b2a6b45-fa0f-5be8-a458-7f177a22e497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18{e}^{28}+24{e}^{-7} \\\\ -18{e}^{28}+18{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355719} :answer-field/id #uuid "b965f82c-b854-52ee-8c41-a50744fb1325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30741167-2bc5-594b-91e9-dac46b63e700" :question/math-academy-id "q-39280" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=7$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2} \\\\ -{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}=6 \\\\ -{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-1$ and adding the result to the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{c}_{1}+4\\cdot 6=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=18.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =18\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(7)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(7) & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4\\cdot 7}+[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-7} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{28}+[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-7} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18{e}^{28}+24{e}^{-7} \\\\ -18{e}^{28}+18{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b63f541-3f34-51d4-a4fb-bf9000ac9064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}-28 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355723} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7097e8-e5cb-5548-a334-56b357801c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355724} {:answer/id #uuid "3a79cc51-8335-521b-ac61-e58fcd8bd7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355725} {:answer/id #uuid "eba85ef1-f68b-5fd5-9c94-00b1a2aa3c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355726} {:answer/id #uuid "5281a488-f6da-5dc7-a137-6deaab9ed062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eba85ef1-f68b-5fd5-9c94-00b1a2aa3c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355726} :answer-field/id #uuid "cef1f508-a2ac-581b-8fbf-86bf08dcbd6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbc7fbb6-5ecf-5e40-98fc-81b2c93a104a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79733" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=4 \\\\ {c}_{1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4\\cdot 3+{c}_{2}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+(-8)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
